        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Gandhism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1908 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mohandas K. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, whose life and work the ideas are drawn from; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-david-thoreau">Henry David Thoreau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a> and John Ruskin among his Western sources; Raychandbhai Mehta, the Jain guru near his home; Vinoba Bhave, who used the term sarvodaya for the post-independence struggle; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>, whom Gandhi named his successor in 1942</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s autobiography, The Story of My Experiments with Truth; Thoreau's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">Civil Disobedience</a> (1849); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Tolstoy</a>'s The Kingdom of God Is Within You (1894); Ruskin's Unto This Last (1862), which Gandhi translated into Gujarati as Sarvodaya in 1908; Gandhi's articles in Indian Opinion and Harijan</span></div>
