    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> did not approve of the term Gandhism and said he had originated no new doctrine; the ideas are therefore derived by others from his life and works, and one such reading rests on truth and nonviolence.</li><li>Satyagraha, the nonviolent resistance he developed, drew on Thoreau's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">Civil Disobedience</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Tolstoy</a>, Ruskin and Indian religious thought, and was later taken up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Lech Wałęsa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san-suu-kyi">Aung San Suu Kyi</a>.</li><li>Gandhians disagreed with one another and with their critics: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s 1918 appeals to Indians to enlist in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> raised doubts among his own supporters, and Jinnah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Savarkar</a> both condemned him over partition, from opposite sides.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1907 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> called Thoreau's logic &quot;incisive&quot; and &quot;unanswerable&quot; in Indian Opinion, and he adopted from Thoreau's essay both the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a> and the means of breaking laws in order to call for their reform.</p></aside>
    </section>

