    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1907</span><span class="rf-text">Thoreau's name first appears in Indian Opinion, the journal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> was editing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1908</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> coins the word sarvodaya as the title of his translation of Ruskin's Unto This Last</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1918</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> issues two public appeals for Indians to enlist in the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1930</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> and his supporters march from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> to Dandi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in defiance of the salt monopoly</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1932</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> fasts against separate electorates for Untouchable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> follows</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1942</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quit-india-movement">Quit India Movement</a> opens civil resistance against British rule</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1948</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> is assassinated</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1957</span><span class="rf-text">A committee to promote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s teaching is formed, and the Gandhi Peace Foundation begins publishing Gandhi Marg</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi.jpg" alt="Mahatma Gandhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahatma-Gandhi,_studio,_1931.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gandhism is a body of ideas that describes the inspiration, vision and life work of Mohandas K. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, particularly his contribution to nonviolent resistance. Gandhi did not approve of the term. He said there was no such thing as Gandhism, that he did not want to leave any sect after him, and that he had simply tried to apply eternal truths to daily life and problems. Because no Gandhism approved by Gandhi exists, one school of thought holds that it has to be derived from his life and works.</p><p>His thought has several earlier influences. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he came under the influence of Henry Stephens Salt, whose A Plea for Vegetarianism turned him to the question of food and brought him to the vegetarian societies, where he found a cause and his first English friends. Thoreau's essay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">Civil Disobedience</a> of 1849 was of greater importance than Walden: Gandhi was already conducting a form of civil disobedience in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> when he read it, and took from it the name and the means of breaking laws to call for their reform. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a>'s critique of institutional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> moved him, and he named his settlement Tolstoy Farm. Ruskin's Unto This Last, given to him by Henry Polak, was a source of Gram Swaraj, his decentralised and self-sufficient economy. His first exploration of pluralism began with the Jain guru Raychandbhai Mehta.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita.jpg" alt="Bhagavad Gita" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BhagavadGita-19th-century-Illustrated-Sanskrit-Chapter_1.20.21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The pivotal element of Gandhism is satya, truth, a virtue of being truthful in thought, speech and action. One deduction from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s life and works takes two principles: first, accepting the truth that people differ at every level, and second, never resorting to violence to settle those differences, whether between two people, two nations, two races or two religions. Satyagraha joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words satya and agraha, to seek or desire, and became widely known during the Indian Independence Movement. Gandhi set out his philosophy in his autobiography, The Story of My Experiments with Truth.</p><p>Nonviolence, ahimsa, has a long history in Indian religious thought. Gandhi wrote that there were causes he was prepared to die for but none he was prepared to kill for. In a letter of July 1918 he nevertheless held that a conception of nonviolence that forbade self-defence was erroneous, and by 1924 he admitted that he lacked the strength of heart to follow the example of Swaminarayan, whom he now admired. He held that fasting brought the body under the mind's control and could cleanse the spirit, and he undertook fasts unto death on three notable occasions. On religion he described his beliefs as rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, held that all religions contain truth and some error, and believed that truth, ahimsa and the Golden Rule lay at the core of every religion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr.jpg" alt="Martin Luther King Jr." loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martin_Luther_King,_Jr._and_Lyndon_Johnson_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yoichi Okamoto, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In his economics <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> favoured simple living and self-sufficiency, with import substitution rather than exports, and pictured a more agrarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> that would meet its people's material needs before it generated wealth or industrialised. His adoption of khadi, homespun cloth, was meant to help end poverty and social and economic discrimination and was a protest against colonial economic policy. He urged members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> to spend time each day at the charkha, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spinning-wheel">spinning wheel</a>, partly to link privileged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and lawyers in Congress with the peasantry, and Motilal Nehru and others gave up Western dress for it. The Khadi movement formed part of the larger swadeshi movement.</p><p>The word sarvodaya, universal uplift, was coined by Gandhi in 1908 as the title of his translation of Ruskin's Unto This Last. After independence Vinoba Bhave used it for the struggle of Gandhians to bring self-determination and equality to the masses. Bhave's associates, including Jaya Prakash Narayan and Dada Dharmadhikari, ran projects of popular self-organisation in the 1950s and 1960s. Gandhians have come from several faiths: Abdul Ghaffar Khan, the "Frontier Gandhi", who organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pathans</a> of the Northwest Frontier as early as 1919; the Briton Horace Alexander; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a>; Hermann Kallenbach; and the atheist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru.jpg" alt="Jawaharlal Nehru" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nehru_in_the_Netherlands,_1957.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harry Pot, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> was assassinated in 1948, and his teachings are held to have played a major role in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s economic and social development and foreign relations for decades. On 15 January 1942 he openly proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> his successor. Nehru was not religious and often disagreed with Gandhi, but he was deeply influenced by him and used his premiership for policies based on Gandhi's principles. His foreign policy was anti-colonial and neutral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, and he helped found the Non-Aligned Movement. He also pushed through legislation granting legal rights to Indian women and outlawing untouchability, against strong opposition from orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>.</p><p>Nehru was not Gandhian in several respects. He refused to condemn the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1956–57, followed a socialist economic model unlike Gandhi's, and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> and Hyderabad into the Indian union through military invasion. Some of his economic policies were criticised for removing the property rights of the landowning peasants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> for whom Gandhi had fought in the early 1920s. Gandhi himself believed in a kind of socialism very different from Nehru's, and called the difference one of emphasis, his on means and Nehru's on ends. Nehru's two biggest failures are thought to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-indian-war">Sino-Indian War</a> of 1962, in which his policy is said to have been influenced by Gandhian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacifism">pacifism</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ali Jinnah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jinnah1945c.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s rigid ahimsa implies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacifism">pacifism</a> and is a source of criticism from across the political spectrum. On partition he wrote in Harijan in October 1946 that the demand for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> was un-Islamic and sinful, yet he also proposed an agreement under which Congress and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> would cooperate to win independence, after which a plebiscite in Muslim-majority districts would decide partition. This double position exposed him to attack from both sides. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> and his contemporaries condemned him for undermining Muslim political rights. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</a> and his allies accused him of appeasing Muslims while overlooking their atrocities against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and of allowing the creation of Pakistan.</p><p>Many Indians condemned his refusal to protest against the executions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagat-singh">Bhagat Singh</a>, Sukhdev, Udham Singh and Rajguru by the colonial government. Economists such as Jagdish Bhagwati have criticised his ideas of swadeshi. His 1932 fast against separate electorates for Untouchable Hindus brought controversy and conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>; both made concessions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a>, which dropped separate electorates in return for voluntary representation and a commitment to abolish untouchability. In July 1940 Gandhi appealed to Britons to meet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> with nonviolence, even at the cost of being slaughtered, and later he answered critics who said nonviolence could not stop Hindu–Muslim riots and that people should arm themselves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere.jpg" alt="Julius Nyerere" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Nyerere_van_Tanzania,_koppen,_Bestanddeelnr_928-2879_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rob Mieremet / Anefo, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s commitment to nonviolent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a> is credited with inspiring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steve-biko">Steve Biko</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Lech Wałęsa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san-suu-kyi">Aung San Suu Kyi</a>. His work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> between 1910 and 1915 for the rights of Indian residents is said to have inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>, which from the 1950s organised nonviolent civil disobedience akin to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> campaigns of the 1920s to 1940s. After the ANC won the first universal elections in 1994, Mandela visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and honoured Gandhi as the man who inspired the freedom struggle of black South Africans. King travelled to India in 1962 to discuss Gandhi's teachings with Nehru, and the protests that followed are linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a>.</p><p>The nonviolent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> overthrew a Soviet-backed communist government in 1989. In Myanmar, Aung San Suu Kyi was placed under house arrest and released in November 2010. In India a committee formed in 1957 to promote Gandhi's teaching recommended books and a Gandhi week for schools, and the Gandhi Peace Foundation has published the journal Gandhi Marg since 1957. A reading of his use of symbols holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-march">Dandi March</a>, the burning of British-made cloth and his fasts were means of spreading his ideas. Gandhi disliked having a cult following and was averse to being called Mahatma.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Gandhism is the body of ideas drawn from Mohandas K. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s life and work, centred on truth and nonviolent resistance, though Gandhi himself rejected the term. It shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s independence movement and Nehru's policies, and inspired civil resistance movements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Myanmar.</em></p>

